pages.content:
In Java, array elements are accessed using **0-based indexing** and the square bracket syntax: `arrayName[index]`.

### Index Calculation & Memory Offset

Because array elements are stored contiguously in memory, the CPU can access any element in **$O(1)$ constant time** using the memory address formula:
$$\text{Element Address} = \text{Base Address} + (\text{Index} \times \text{Element Size})$$

```java
public class ArrayAccessDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] languages = {"Java", "Python", "C++", "Rust", "Go"};

        // First element is at index 0
        System.out.println("First: " + languages[0]); // Java

        // Third element is at index 2
        System.out.println("Third: " + languages[2]); // C++

        // Last element is at index (length - 1)
        System.out.println("Last: " + languages[languages.length - 1]); // Go
    }
}
```

### ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
If you attempt to access an index that is negative or greater than or equal to the array length, the JVM throws an unchecked runtime exception:

```java
int[] scores = {90, 85, 78};
// System.out.println(scores[3]); // RUNTIME ERROR: ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException: Index 3 out of bounds for length 3
// System.out.println(scores[-1]); // RUNTIME ERROR: Index -1 out of bounds
```

