pages.content:
Although an array's length is immutable, its **individual elements are fully mutable**. You can update any element by assigning a new value to its specific index.

### Updating Array Elements

```java
public class ArrayUpdateDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] temperatures = {22, 24, 21, 25, 23};

        System.out.println("Original Day 3 temp: " + temperatures[2]); // 21

        // Update Day 3 temperature
        temperatures[2] = 27;
        System.out.println("Updated Day 3 temp: " + temperatures[2]);  // 27

        // In-place increment
        temperatures[0] += 5; // Increases 22 to 27
    }
}
```

### Copying Arrays Safely
Assigning one array variable to another (`arr2 = arr1;`) **does not** create a new array; it only copies the reference pointer! To create an independent copy:

```java
// Method 1: System.arraycopy
int[] copy1 = new int[temperatures.length];
System.arraycopy(temperatures, 0, copy1, 0, temperatures.length);

// Method 2: Arrays.copyOf
int[] copy2 = java.util.Arrays.copyOf(temperatures, temperatures.length);
```

