pages.content:
When an array is created using `new type[size]`, the JVM automatically zeroes out the memory block, assigning **default values** based on the component type.

### Java Array Default Values Table

| Data Type | Default Value | Example Initialization |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`byte`, `short`, `int`, `long`** | `0` (or `0L`) | `new int[3]` $\rightarrow$ `[0, 0, 0]` |
| **`float`, `double`** | `0.0f`, `0.0d` | `new double[3]` $\rightarrow$ `[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]` |
| **`boolean`** | `false` | `new boolean[3]` $\rightarrow$ `[false, false, false]` |
| **`char`** | `'\u0000'` (null char) | `new char[3]` $\rightarrow$ `['\0', '\0', '\0']` |
| **Reference Types (`String`, objects)**| `null` | `new String[3]` $\rightarrow$ `[null, null, null]` |

```java
public class DefaultValuesDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] numbers = new int[2];
        boolean[] flags = new boolean[2];
        String[] words = new String[2];

        System.out.println("int: " + numbers[0]);     // 0
        System.out.println("boolean: " + flags[0]);  // false
        System.out.println("String: " + words[0]);   // null
    }
}
```

> [!WARNING]
> While local primitive variables must be explicitly initialized before use, array elements are **always guaranteed** to receive default values upon heap allocation.

