pages.content:
In real-world applications, array sizes and elements are often provided dynamically at runtime by users or external data streams.

### Dynamic Array Input with Scanner

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class DynamicArrayInput {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter number of items: ");
        int size = sc.nextInt(); // Read dynamic size

        int[] data = new int[size]; // Dynamically allocate array

        System.out.println("Enter " + size + " integer values:");
        for (int i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {
            data[i] = sc.nextInt();
        }

        System.out.println("Array populated successfully:");
        for (int val : data) {
            System.out.print(val + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();

        sc.close();
    }
}
```

### Input Validation Best Practice
Always verify that the user enters a non-negative size. Passing a negative number to array allocation (`new int[-5]`) throws `NegativeArraySizeException` at runtime.

