pages.content:
An **ArrayList** in Java is a resizable-array implementation of the `List` interface in the `java.util` package. Unlike standard arrays with fixed capacity, an `ArrayList` grows and shrinks dynamically as elements are added or removed.

### Generic ArrayList Initialization

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class ArrayListInitDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Preferred: Program to the List interface
        List<String> cities = new ArrayList<>();

        // Adding elements using add()
        cities.add("Tokyo");
        cities.add("New York");
        cities.add("London");

        System.out.println("Cities: " + cities);
    }
}
```

### How ArrayList Resizes Automatically
1. **Initial Capacity:** When created with `new ArrayList<>()`, it has a default initial capacity of **10** (allocated upon first addition).
2. **Growth Formula:** When the internal array becomes full, the ArrayList allocates a new underlying array with capacity:
   $$\text{New Capacity} = \text{Old Capacity} + (\text{Old Capacity} \gg 1) \approx 1.5 \times \text{Old Capacity}$$
3. **Element Transfer:** Existing elements are copied into the new array using `Arrays.copyOf()`.

> [!TIP]
> If you know in advance that an ArrayList will hold 10,000 elements, instantiate it with `new ArrayList<>(10000)` to avoid expensive multiple internal resizing operations.

