pages.content:
To modify an existing element in an `ArrayList`, use the **`.set(int index, E element)`** method.

### Syntax of .set()

```java
E previousElement = list.set(int index, E newElement);
```

- **`index`**: The position of the element to replace.
- **`newElement`**: The new value to store at that position.
- **Return Value**: The method returns the **previous element** that was replaced.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class ArrayListUpdateDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<Integer> scores = new ArrayList<>();
        scores.add(80);
        scores.add(90);
        scores.add(70);

        System.out.println("Before: " + scores); // [80, 90, 70]

        // Replace index 1 (90) with 95
        int oldVal = scores.set(1, 95);

        System.out.println("Replaced value: " + oldVal); // 90
        System.out.println("After: " + scores);          // [80, 95, 70]
    }
}
```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Difference between `.set()` and `.add()`:**
> - `.set(index, element)` **replaces** the existing element; size remains unchanged.
> - `.add(index, element)` **inserts** an element at that index, shifting existing elements to the right; size increases by 1.

