pages.content:
The **`.size()`** method returns the number of elements currently stored in the `ArrayList`.

### Size vs Capacity

It is vital to understand the difference between **size** and **capacity**:
- **Size (`.size()`):** The count of actual elements currently inside the list.
- **Capacity:** The length of the internal array buffer allocated in heap memory. Capacity is managed automatically by Java.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class SizeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> tasks = new ArrayList<>();

        System.out.println("Initial size: " + tasks.size()); // 0
        System.out.println("Is empty: " + tasks.isEmpty());  // true

        tasks.add("Design");
        tasks.add("Develop");
        tasks.add("Deploy");

        System.out.println("Current size: " + tasks.size()); // 3
        System.out.println("Is empty: " + tasks.isEmpty());  // false
    }
}
```

> [!NOTE]
> Do not confuse array's `.length` property with ArrayList's `.size()` method or String's `.length()` method!

