pages.content:
Because an `ArrayList` is dynamically sized, you can continuously accept user input without knowing the total count of elements in advance.

### Sentinel-Controlled ArrayList Input

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class DynamicListInput {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        ArrayList<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();

        System.out.println("Enter numbers (enter -1 to finish):");
        while (true) {
            int input = sc.nextInt();
            if (input == -1) {
                break; // Sentinel stop value
            }
            numbers.add(input); // Autoboxed int -> Integer
        }

        System.out.println("Total numbers entered: " + numbers.size());
        System.out.println("Elements: " + numbers);

        sc.close();
    }
}
```

In the example above, the list seamlessly accommodates 0, 5, or 50,000 numbers without any manual memory management or pre-allocation.

