pages.content:
Java `ArrayList` provides two distinct overloads for removing elements: **removal by index** and **removal by value**.

### 1. Removal by Index: `remove(int index)`
Removes the element at the specified index and shifts all subsequent elements to the left. It **returns the removed element**.

### 2. Removal by Value: `remove(Object o)`
Scans from left to right using `.equals()`, removes the **first occurrence** of the object, and **returns a boolean** (`true` if found and removed, `false` otherwise).

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class RemoveOverloadsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();
        numbers.add(10);
        numbers.add(20);
        numbers.add(30);
        numbers.add(20);

        // Trap Alert: numbers.remove(20) calls remove(int index), throwing IndexOutOfBoundsException!
        // To remove the VALUE 20:
        numbers.remove(Integer.valueOf(20)); // Removes first 20 at index 1
        System.out.println("After removing value 20: " + numbers); // [10, 30, 20]

        // Remove element at index 0:
        int removedVal = numbers.remove(0);
        System.out.println("Removed: " + removedVal + " | List: " + numbers); // Removed: 10 | List: [30, 20]
    }
}
```

