pages.content:
To verify membership in an `ArrayList`, Java provides the **`.contains(Object o)`** and **`.indexOf(Object o)`** methods.

### Search Methods

- **`contains(Object o)`**: Returns `true` if the list contains at least one element equal to `o` (via `o.equals(element)`), otherwise `false`.
- **`indexOf(Object o)`**: Returns the index of the first occurrence of `o`, or **`-1`** if the list does not contain it.
- **`lastIndexOf(Object o)`**: Returns the index of the last occurrence of `o`, or `-1`.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class SearchDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
        names.add("Alice");
        names.add("Bob");
        names.add("Charlie");
        names.add("Alice");

        System.out.println("Contains Bob: " + names.contains("Bob"));       // true
        System.out.println("Contains David: " + names.contains("David"));   // false
        System.out.println("First Alice index: " + names.indexOf("Alice")); // 0
        System.out.println("Last Alice index: " + names.lastIndexOf("Alice")); // 3
        System.out.println("David index: " + names.indexOf("David"));       // -1
    }
}
```

> [!NOTE]
> Both `contains()` and `indexOf()` perform a linear search from index 0, taking **$O(n)$ time complexity**.

