pages.content:
The **`isEmpty()`** method provides a clean, self-documenting way to check if an `ArrayList` contains zero elements.

### Clean Code: `isEmpty()` vs `size() == 0`

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class IsEmptyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<Integer> buffer = new ArrayList<>();

        if (buffer.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Buffer is empty. Ready for new incoming batch.");
        }

        buffer.add(42);

        if (!buffer.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Buffer contains " + buffer.size() + " item(s).");
        }
    }
}
```

**Why prefer `isEmpty()`?**
- **Readability:** `if (list.isEmpty())` directly states the business intent.
- **Abstraction:** Belongs to the root `java.util.Collection` interface, so code refactors cleanly between `ArrayList`, `HashSet`, and `LinkedList`.

