pages.content:
The **`.clear()`** method removes all elements from an `ArrayList`, resetting its size to zero.

### Internal Mechanics of `clear()`

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class ClearDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList<String> cache = new ArrayList<>();
        cache.add("Session_1");
        cache.add("Session_2");

        System.out.println("Size before clear: " + cache.size()); // 2

        cache.clear();

        System.out.println("Size after clear: " + cache.size());   // 0
        System.out.println("Is empty: " + cache.isEmpty());        // true
    }
}
```

### Memory Cleanup
When `clear()` executes, the JVM iterates through the internal array and sets every slot to `null`. This permits the **Garbage Collector** to reclaim objects that were referenced only by this list. The allocated capacity of the internal buffer is retained for future insertions.

