pages.content:
In Java, a **two-dimensional (2D) array** is technically an **array of arrays**. Each row in a 2D array is an independent 1D array object on the JVM heap.

### Static and Dynamic 2D Array Initializations

```java
public class MatrixInitDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 1. Static Initialization: Values declared inline
        int[][] matrix = {
            {1, 2, 3},
            {4, 5, 6}
        }; // 2 rows, 3 columns

        // 2. Dynamic Initialization: Allocate dimensions
        int rows = 3;
        int cols = 4;
        int[][] grid = new int[rows][cols]; // 3 rows, 4 columns (all initialized to 0)

        // Access dimensions:
        System.out.println("Number of rows: " + matrix.length);       // 2
        System.out.println("Number of columns: " + matrix[0].length); // 3
    }
}
```

### Jagged / Ragged Arrays in Java
Because each row is an independent array reference, Java natively supports **jagged arrays** where different rows have differing lengths:

```java
int[][] jagged = new int[3][]; // Declare 3 rows without column sizes
jagged[0] = new int[2]; // Row 0 has 2 columns
jagged[1] = new int[4]; // Row 1 has 4 columns
jagged[2] = new int[3]; // Row 2 has 3 columns
```

